# InboxLabeler web application — configuration
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is the
# committed template and must never hold a real value.
#
# Three parts of the application read this file: the remote MCP endpoint with its
# OAuth flow, the website's own sign-in and signed-in pages, and the static legal pages
# at /privacy, /terms and /impressum. The signed-out landing page reads none of it and
# renders the same either way.
#
#   Variable               Development            Production
#   ---------------------- ---------------------- ------------------------------
#   PUBLIC_ORIGIN         optional               REQUIRED
#   OAUTH_SIGNING_SECRET   required               REQUIRED   ← secret
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID       required to sign in    REQUIRED
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET   required to sign in    REQUIRED   ← secret
#   DATABASE_URL           optional               REQUIRED   ← secret
#   ALLOWED_EMAILS         optional               REQUIRED — see below
#   LEGAL_NAME             optional               REQUIRED for /privacy etc.
#   LEGAL_ADDRESS_LINE_1   optional               REQUIRED for /privacy etc.
#   LEGAL_ADDRESS_LINE_2   optional               REQUIRED for /privacy etc.
#   LEGAL_CONTACT_EMAIL    optional               REQUIRED for /privacy etc.
#   DEV_DATABASE_DIR       optional               unused
#
# The three marked secret must be set as encrypted environment variables on the
# host — on Vercel, Project Settings → Environment Variables — and never
# committed, never printed, and never pasted into a support thread. The others
# are not secret but are still per-environment.
#
# Scope them to Production. A platform that gives every deployment its own
# hostname will otherwise run this authorization server at several addresses at
# once, all signing with the same key and writing to the same database, while the
# discovery documents name one. Give previews their own database branch, their own
# secret and their own Google client, or leave them unset — a deployment with no
# secrets refuses the requests that need them, which is the right answer. The
# OAuth and MCP endpoints also refuse any request whose Host is not the configured
# origin's, so a preview that did inherit production secrets still cannot run a
# flow.
#
# A production deployment missing a variable the OAuth flow or /mcp requires
# refuses the requests that need it and writes the reason to stderr. It never
# falls back to a development default: the message goes to the log, never to the
# client.
#
# The LEGAL_* variables behave differently, on purpose. Their absence is not a
# fault to log: with any of them unset, /privacy, /terms and /impressum answer 404
# rather than render a disclosure that names nobody, and nothing else changes.
# Those pages are prerendered, so the values take effect on the next build —
# configure them and redeploy.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where this deployment is reached
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The public origin this deployment is reached at — scheme, host and port, and
# nothing else:
#
#   PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://inboxlabeler.com
#
# Every OAuth and MCP URL is derived by appending to it: the issuer identifier,
# the MCP endpoint at /mcp, the authorize, token, register and callback
# endpoints, and both discovery documents. So it is the origin, NOT the URL of
# the MCP endpoint — do not write .../mcp here.
#
# A path, query or fragment is refused rather than dropped, because dropping one
# would serve the whole OAuth surface from somewhere nobody configured and
# nothing would say so until a client failed. A trailing slash is fine: it is the
# same origin.
#
# It is configured rather than taken from the request's Host header on purpose.
# A token's audience and the issuer are values a client compares literally, and
# Host is attacker-controlled — deriving them from it would let a forged Host
# mint a token an honest client accepts for a different server.
#
# Optional in development, where it defaults to http://localhost:3000.
# Required in production, and it must be the canonical origin clients will use —
# not a per-deployment hostname. The issuer and a token's audience are compared
# literally, so a preview URL would mint tokens no client accepts at the real one.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Token signing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The secret access tokens are signed with, and the key the opaque per-user
# reference is derived from. At least 32 bytes; anything shorter is rejected
# rather than padded, because a stretched weak secret leaves tokens forgeable
# while the deployment looks configured.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Changing it invalidates every access token already issued, which is also the
# way to revoke them all at once.
OAUTH_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google, as the identity provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# InboxLabeler issues its own tokens for its own MCP endpoint, but does not
# authenticate people itself: it hands that one step to Google and gets back a
# stable account subject. The scopes are `openid email` — the address is used to
# check ALLOWED_EMAILS below, and then dropped everywhere except a browser
# session, which keeps it for as long as that session lasts so the site can
# say which Google account it is showing. `profile` is not requested.
#
# There are TWO redirect URIs, because there are two flows that come back from
# Google: an MCP client being authorized, and a person signing in to the website.
# BOTH have to be registered, or the one you forgot fails with Google's
# `redirect_uri_mismatch` and nothing else says why.
#
# Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID of type "Web application" at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials, then add, exactly:
#
#   Authorised JavaScript origin   https://inboxlabeler.com
#   Authorised redirect URI        https://inboxlabeler.com/oauth/callback
#   Authorised redirect URI        https://inboxlabeler.com/auth/callback
#
# and, for a local checkout, the development set alongside them:
#
#   Authorised JavaScript origin   http://localhost:3000
#   Authorised redirect URI        http://localhost:3000/oauth/callback
#   Authorised redirect URI        http://localhost:3000/auth/callback
#
# Both redirect URIs must match PUBLIC_ORIGIN, because they are derived from it
# rather than written down anywhere in the application. One Google client can hold
# both sets, so development and production may share it — or not, which is tidier.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET is a secret.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may sign in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A comma-separated list of the email addresses allowed to authenticate — the
# whole of InboxLabeler's access control, and how a private beta is closed.
#
#   ALLOWED_EMAILS=georg@example.com,tester@example.com
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. There are three behaviours, and the third is the one
# worth reading twice:
#
#   configured        only the listed addresses may authenticate. Everyone else
#                     is refused at the identity boundary, having authenticated
#                     with Google successfully and received nothing from us.
#
#   unset or empty,   any Google account with a verified email address may
#   in development    authenticate. A checkout has to work after `npm install`
#                     without a list to maintain, and a developer signing in to
#                     their own machine is not an access-control question.
#
#   unset or empty,   NOBODY is admitted. The deployment refuses every sign-in
#   in production     and writes the reason to stderr, exactly as it does for a
#                     missing signing secret or a missing database.
#
# The third behaviour is the point of the variable being required. A closed beta
# must not become public because a variable was renamed, blanked, or lost on the
# way into an environment — so "no list" reads as "no list of who may come in",
# and the only safe answer to that is nobody. Every way of having no list is the
# same fault: unset, "", " ", ",", " , , ".
#
# It refuses rather than answering "you are not on the list", because there is no
# list: that answer would be a lie told to somebody who may well be invited, and
# it would leave you with a working-looking deployment that nobody can use and
# nothing to say why. Removing the last address therefore closes the deployment
# rather than opening it — which also means a signed-in browser loses access
# on its next page load.
#
# Matching ignores case and surrounding space on both sides, in every environment:
# one rule, wherever it runs. The `email` scope is requested so the address can be
# read, and Google must have marked it verified — an unverified address is one the
# account holder typed rather than proved, so a list checked against it would be
# one anyone could join.
#
# The address decides *access* only. The user's identity stays the Google subject,
# which survives them changing their address; no token and no MCP result carries
# the address. The one place it is stored is a browser session row, which the
# site reads to show whose account is signed in and to re-ask this list on
# every request — so taking an address off the list closes the site to that
# browser on its next page load. It is deleted with the session.
ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who operates this deployment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The name, postal address and contact address shown on /impressum, /privacy and
# /terms. Configuration rather than source, because for a deployment run by an
# individual these are a person's name and home address, and a repository is
# public, cloned and mirrored.
#
# The address must be one at which the operator can actually be served — a post
# office box does not satisfy § 5 DDG. Line 1 is the street and number, line 2
# the postcode and place; the country is stated by the pages themselves.
#
# All four are required together. With any of them unset or blank, /impressum,
# /privacy and /terms answer 404 rather than render an incomplete disclosure: a
# page naming nobody looks like the required statement without being one. Every
# other route is unaffected — the OAuth endpoints and /mcp neither read nor need
# these.
#
#   LEGAL_NAME=Erika Mustermann
#   LEGAL_ADDRESS_LINE_1=Musterstrasse 1
#   LEGAL_ADDRESS_LINE_2=12345 Musterstadt
#   LEGAL_CONTACT_EMAIL=support@example.com
#
# Not secret — they are published on those pages — but still per-deployment.
LEGAL_NAME=
LEGAL_ADDRESS_LINE_1=
LEGAL_ADDRESS_LINE_2=
LEGAL_CONTACT_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Durable storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres, holding all three hosted schemas: the OAuth flow state (registered
# clients, logins in progress, authorization codes, refresh tokens), each user's
# own labels and match history, and the website's browser sessions. They share a
# connection and nothing else — separate tables, separate migrations. Access
# tokens are in none of them: they are signed and self-contained, so /mcp
# validates one without a lookup. A browser session is the opposite on purpose,
# because signing out has to revoke rather than merely ask a browser to forget.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION, and a secret. Without it a hosted deployment refuses
# the requests that need it rather than falling back, because a store one restart
# or one extra instance can invalidate is the failure durability exists to remove.
#
# On Neon, use the POOLED connection string — the host containing "-pooler". Each
# serverless instance keeps its own small pool, so many instances against the
# direct endpoint would exhaust the compute's connection limit; the pooler exists
# for exactly that shape. The connection string Neon offers already carries
# `?sslmode=require`, which is what turns TLS on: keep it.
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/inboxlabeler?sslmode=require
#
# Initialise or update the schema with:  npm run db:migrate
#
# IN PRODUCTION THIS IS A REQUIRED DEPLOY STEP, not a convenience. A production
# instance checks that the schema it needs has been applied and refuses the
# requests that need it otherwise; it never runs DDL itself. A request from the
# internet must not be what migrates a database — the first request after a deploy
# is an arbitrary one, several instances would race to be it, and a migration
# failing halfway would do so inside somebody's page load with nobody watching.
#
# So the order is: run the migration against this DATABASE_URL, then deploy the
# code that needs it. Outside production the schema is still brought up to date
# automatically, so a checkout needs no extra step.
# DATABASE_URL=

# Development only: where the embedded database keeps its files (all three schemas).
# Unset means in-memory, emptied when the process stops; set it to keep
# registrations, logins and labels across a restart the way production does.
# Ignored entirely when DATABASE_URL is set.
DEV_DATABASE_DIR=.devdb
